\section{TDMM}
TDMM: Bayesian Time-Dynamic Models with Optional Mixed Effects fits Gaussian,
Bernoulli, and Poisson responses with regression coefficients that vary
smoothly over time. With \verb|random_intercept = TRUE| (the default), a
subject-specific random intercept supports repeated observations at irregular
times and unequal visit counts. With \verb|random_intercept = FALSE|, the
model supports independent observations; time may represent age, time since
an event, or time relative to an event. Numeric covariates may be
constant within subject (baseline) or change at each visit. The default is
eight cubic B-spline basis functions (polynomial degree 3, order 4) with a
second-order coefficient-difference penalty. JAGS is required for fitting;
TDMM constructs the model matrices and selects the JAGS model internally.

\section{Data and the fitting workflow}
For analysis, provide one row per observation. The subject ID,
numeric observation time, response, and numeric covariates must be separate
columns. In the example below, \verb|baseline| is repeated within subject
and \verb|changing| varies by visit. The response is Gaussian, and the
coefficient functions are known because the data are simulated.

<<gaussian-data,echo=TRUE>>=
set.seed(27181)
truth <- function(t) cbind(beta0 = sin(2*pi*t),
                           beta1 = cos(2*pi*t),
                           beta2 = 1.5*(t-0.5))
dat <- do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(40), function(i) {
  n <- sample(5:8, 1)
  time <- sort(runif(n))
  baseline <- rnorm(1)
  changing <- sin(2*pi*time) + rnorm(n, sd=0.25)
  random_intercept <- rnorm(1, sd=sqrt(0.5))
  beta <- truth(time)
  y <- rnorm(n, beta[,1] + baseline*beta[,2] +
            changing*beta[,3] + random_intercept, sd=sqrt(0.1))
  data.frame(id=paste0('person-',i), time=time, y=y,
             baseline=baseline, changing=changing)
}))
head(dat, 3)
c(subjects=length(unique(dat$id)), observations=nrow(dat),
  min_visits=min(table(dat$id)), max_visits=max(table(dat$id)))
@

The recommended workflow is \verb|data -> tdmm() -> summary() -> plot()|,
followed by convergence diagnostics. After installing JAGS, fit the simulated
data with the following call. Adjust the MCMC settings and review diagnostics
before drawing conclusions from your own data.

\verb|random_intercept = TRUE| is the default and includes a subject-specific
random intercept, as in this example. For independent observations, use
\verb|random_intercept = FALSE| to omit it. In that setting, time may
represent age, time since an event, or time relative to an event.

\begin{verbatim}
library(TDMM)
fit <- tdmm(dat, family='gaussian', subject.var='id',
            time.var='time', y.var='y',
            x.var=c('baseline','changing'), n_basis=8,
            random_intercept=TRUE,
            control=tdmm_control(chains=3, iterations=4000,
              burn_in=1500, thin=2, adapt=2000, seed=77181),
            priors=tdmm.priors('gaussian'))
summary(fit)
plot(fit)
plot.trace.tdmm(fit, c('sigma.b','sigma2.e','tau.alpha[1]'))
\end{verbatim}

The coefficient curves are on the Gaussian response scale; Bernoulli curves
are on the log-odds scale and Poisson curves on the log-mean scale. In the
Gaussian example, \verb|beta0| is the intercept, \verb|beta1| multiplies
\verb|baseline|, and \verb|beta2| multiplies \verb|changing|, in the order
passed to \verb|x.var|. \verb|summary(fit)| returns a list invisibly as well
as printing a concise summary. \verb|plot(fit)| reconstructs curves on a
grid consistent with the fitted spline specification.

\subsection{Coefficient-function recovery}
The figure illustrates coefficient-function recovery from a larger Gaussian
simulation with the same known coefficient functions. The posterior mean
curves follow the true functions across time. Dashed lines show the truth;
shaded regions show pointwise 95\% credible bands.

<<gaussian-curves,echo=TRUE,fig=TRUE,width=9,height=3.4>>=
example_data_path <- function(filename) {
  path <- system.file('extdata', filename, package='TDMM')
  if (!nzchar(path)) path <- file.path('..','inst','extdata',filename)
  stopifnot(file.exists(path))
  path
}
g <- read.csv(example_data_path('gaussian-pointwise.csv'))
oldpar <- par(mfrow=c(1,3), mar=c(4,4,2,1))
for (nm in c('beta0','beta1','beta2')) {
  z <- g[g$coefficient==nm,]
  plot(z$time,z$estimate,type='n',ylim=range(z$truth,z$lower,z$upper),
       xlab='Time',ylab=nm,main=nm)
  polygon(c(z$time,rev(z$time)),c(z$lower,rev(z$upper)),
          col='gray90',border=NA)
  lines(z$time,z$estimate,lwd=2,col='navy')
  lines(z$time,z$truth,lty=2,lwd=1.5)
}
par(oldpar)
@

Before interpreting a fit, inspect chain trace plots, R-hat, and effective
sample size (ESS) for the reconstructed coefficient functions and relevant
model parameters. Review the uncertainty bands and any sampler warnings in
the context of the observed data.

\section{PBC2: a real-data application}
This application uses \verb|JMbayes2::pbc2|: 1,945 observations from
312 subjects, with 1--16 visits each. The response is log serum bilirubin,
time is years since baseline, and the covariates are a baseline treatment
indicator and longitudinal albumin. Treatment is coded 0 for placebo and 1
for D-penicillamine. Albumin is centered at the overall
\emph{observation-level} mean (3.3898868894601542 g/dL), not the mean of
subject means. Thus the intercept represents the modeled placebo trajectory
at that albumin level and random intercept zero.

The following code shows the preprocessing and the \verb|tdmm()| fit.
Install \verb|JMbayes2| to obtain the dataset.

\begin{verbatim}
install.packages('JMbayes2') # if needed
raw <- new.env(parent=emptyenv())
utils::data('pbc2', package='JMbayes2', envir=raw)
pbc2 <- raw$pbc2
albumin_center <- mean(pbc2$albumin) # 3.3898868894601542
pbc <- data.frame(
  id=as.numeric(pbc2$id), time=pbc2$year,
  y=log(pbc2$serBilir),
  drug=as.integer(pbc2$drug=='D-penicil'),
  albumin=pbc2$albumin-albumin_center)
fit_pbc <- tdmm(pbc, family='gaussian', subject.var='id',
  time.var='time', y.var='y', x.var=c('drug','albumin'),
  degree=3, n_basis=8, random_intercept=TRUE,
  control=tdmm_control(chains=3, iterations=8000,
    burn_in=3000, thin=5, adapt=5000, seed=541),
  priors=tdmm.priors('gaussian'))
summary(fit_pbc)
plot(fit_pbc)
plot.trace.tdmm(fit_pbc, c('sigma.b','sigma2.e','tau.alpha[1]'))
\end{verbatim}

The treatment 95\% credible interval includes zero throughout the
common evaluation grid. The albumin coefficient is negative, with its 95\%
band below zero throughout that grid. The time-varying intercept generally
rises through the better-supported follow-up period. These are conditional
associations; the treatment curve does not establish a causal effect. Late
follow-up is sparse, so small fluctuations in the tail should not be read as
precise clinical changes.

<<pbc-curves,echo=TRUE,fig=TRUE,width=9,height=3.4>>=
p <- read.csv(example_data_path('pbc2-curves.csv'))
p <- p[p$n_basis==8,]
oldpar <- par(mfrow=c(1,3), mar=c(4,4,2,1))
for (nm in c('intercept','treatment','albumin')) {
  z <- p[p$coefficient==nm,]
  plot(z$time,z$estimate,type='n',ylim=range(z$lower,z$upper,0),
       xlab='Years since baseline',ylab='Coefficient',main=nm)
  polygon(c(z$time,rev(z$time)),c(z$lower,rev(z$upper)),
          col='gray90',border=NA)
  abline(h=0,col='gray60',lty=3)
  lines(z$time,z$estimate,lwd=2,col='navy')
}
par(oldpar)
@

The eight-basis fit had maximum curve R-hat 1.0008 and minimum curve ESS
2,834. A basis sensitivity check held the data, priors, and controls fixed
while comparing 6, 8, and 10 basis functions; the broad curves, intervals,
and substantive conclusions were stable. This is robustness assessment, not
selection of the most favorable result.

\section{Using your own data and sensitivity analysis}
Replace the column names and family in the template below. Provide covariates
in the intended coefficient order; with repeated observations, a covariate can
be baseline or measured at each visit. The data checks reject missing analysis values; decide how
to handle missing data before fitting rather than relying on implicit imputation.

\begin{verbatim}
fit <- tdmm(my_long_data, family='poisson',
  subject.var='participant_id', time.var='visit_time',
  y.var='count', x.var=c('baseline_x','changing_x'),
  n_basis=8, random_intercept=TRUE,
  control=tdmm_control(chains=3, iterations=8000,
    burn_in=3000, thin=5, adapt=5000, seed=123),
  priors=tdmm.priors('poisson'))
summary(fit)
plot(fit)
\end{verbatim}

It is reasonable to vary \verb|n_basis| (eight is the default) and, for
advanced sensitivity analyses, smoothing-prior parameters. Consider
random-effect or residual-prior sensitivity when scientifically important.
TDMM handles basis construction and model setup internally. For Bernoulli or
Poisson outcomes, use the same workflow with the corresponding \verb|family|
value. Short examples for both families are in the repository's
\verb|examples| folder.

To use independent chains on macOS or Linux, use the same fitter with:
\begin{verbatim}
control=tdmm_control(parallel=TRUE, cores=2, ...)
\end{verbatim}
The current parallel backend uses forked workers and is not implemented for
Windows.

\end{document}
